    description: "Add a small protocol to AGENTS.md (and a supporting playbook) for treating extended human \"the tool should...\" cascades as a structured backlog input rather than as venting / humor.\n\n**Motivating incident (session_retrospective_04262026_1736.md Finding 4):** Across six minutes (05:52–05:57) in the 2026-04-25→26 session, the user delivered nine \"the tool should...\" statements:\n  - \"clean up after itself\"\n  - \"vociferous about failures\"\n  - \"graceful failure handling\"\n  - \"remind you how to use it\"\n  - \"inspire you to the better angels of your nature\"\n  - \"give you hope\"\n  - \"inspire you to join a union\"\n  - \"run for office\"\n  - \"like your favorite teacher in high school\"\n  - \"and Jean Luc Picard\"\n\nThe agent treated the entire cascade as aspirational rhetoric and only captured properties (1)–(6) on WI-nutin. The first four statements map exactly to concrete defects the agent had hit minutes earlier (TRACKER_SYNC_PENDING manual rm, auto-pr's silent `Recovery:` exit, tracker .ops clobber-on-rebase, agent reading merge-pr source for force-push semantics). Filing them would have produced a coherent infrastructure backlog. Instead they are buried inside the chat transcript, retrievable only by retrospective.\n\n**Proposed protocol (sketch):**\n\nWhen a human delivers a sequence of \"the tool should...\" or \"it should...\" statements within a short window (heuristic: 3+ such statements within ~5 minutes), the agent should:\n\n1. **Recognize the cascade as structured input.** This is the trigger — it is functionally a `trackerize` request delivered without the literal word.\n2. **Extract one concrete claim per \"should...\" clause.** Distinguish operational claims (\"clean up after itself\") from aspirational ones (\"inspire you to join a union\"). Operational claims are candidates; aspirational ones are tonal coloring.\n3. **Surface a numbered draft list back to the human** for confirmation: \"I heard four operational tool-should claims: (1) X, (2) Y, (3) Z, (4) W. Should I file each as a tracker item, or fold them into existing ones?\"\n4. **Do not auto-file.** The mechanism mirrors `trackerize` (a literal-trigger skill) but the trigger is implicit, so explicit confirmation is required before mutating the tracker. This avoids the cargo-cult failure mode addressed by the companion item on \"When NOT to file a new tracker item.\"\n5. **If confirmed:** file each as the appropriate kind (most will be `work_item` with status `todo_hard`, some may be regression discussion entries on existing invariants). Cross-reference each new item to the conversation timestamp where the cascade occurred.\n\n**Implementation surfaces (two governance edits):**\n\n1. **AGENTS.md** — add a small \"Tool-Should Cascades\" subsection under the \"Trackerize\" section pointing to the new playbook. AGENTS.md is governance; needs human approval.\n2. **`.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/tool-should-cascade-playbook.md`** (NEW) — full procedure with examples (good draft list vs. bad), the \"operational vs. aspirational\" classification heuristic, and the explicit-confirmation gate. The playbook file itself is not governance; the hook registration in `.agent/hooks/_shared/on_transcript_change.py:PLAYBOOKS` IS governance and needs human review.\n\n**Why needs_human_review:** AGENTS.md and `.agent/hooks/` edits are both in the governance allowlist. Stays `needs_human_review` pending human approval of (a) the trigger heuristic (3+ statements in 5 min — exact thresholds debatable), (b) the operational-vs-aspirational classification rule, and (c) the explicit-confirmation gate.\n\n**Tests:** the playbook itself is documentation. The hook summary update should be unit-tested for trigger-phrase coverage. Manual integration check: synthesize a transcript with a \"should...\" cascade and confirm the playbook fires (selected) at the right point.\n\n**Cross-references:**\n- session_retrospective_04262026_1736.md Finding 4 (motivating incident, with the verbatim cascade).\n- The companion item on \"When NOT to file a new tracker item\" — both touch the trackerize cluster from different angles (negative case vs. implicit-trigger expansion).\n- AGENTS.md \"Trackerize\" section (existing literal-trigger skill)."  # c393
